# AGENTS.md

Repository rules for `<%= name %>`. These apply to every contributor, human or AI-assisted.
Adding a dependency or a tool, the tests' rules and the release are
[CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md)'s.

## 1. Lints and Types

RuboCop runs with its defaults, a few cops beyond them, and every new cop as it ships
(`NewCops: enable`), its Minitest, packaging, performance and Rake extensions on, and the project
indexed first (`UseProjectIndex`, the rubydex gem) for the cops that look across files
(`.rubocop.yml`). Steep checks `lib/` against the signatures in `sig/`, its strictest diagnostics on
(`Steepfile`): every method of `lib/` is declared in `sig/`, and a warning fails as an error does.
Gemfile.lock pins both, so the verdict is the same locally and in CI. YARD documents `lib/`, and
yard-lint holds it (`.yard-lint.yml`).

The shell is held as the code is: shfmt formats it (`.editorconfig` holds its style) and it passes
ShellCheck. The Nix is nixfmt's. The workflows pass actionlint and, with Dependabot's
configuration, zizmor's security audit.

### 1.1. RuboCop

- Before finishing, check what you changed with `bundle exec rubocop --changed --format json`.
- Fix offenses with `bundle exec rubocop -a FILES`. Don't use `-A` unless asked.
- If an offense is unclear, run `bundle exec rubocop --explain Department/CopName`.
- Don't edit `.rubocop.yml` to make offenses go away.
- When an offense has to stay, disable the cop on that line, or the next (`# rubocop:disable-next`
  on the line above), and say why:
  `# rubocop:disable Department/CopName -- the reason`.

### 1.2. YARD

- Every public object of `lib/` is documented (yard-lint, at 100% coverage; `.yardopts` keeps the
  public API): what it is for, then its tags -- each `@param`, `@return`, `@yield` and `@raise` --
  their types the signatures' in YARD's syntax (`Array<String>`, `Hash{Symbol => String}`,
  `String, nil`). A private one takes a comment only as §2 has it, and no tags: its signature
  holds its types.
- `bundle exec rake yard_lint` lints it (`yard-lint lib --stats`); `bundle exec rake yard` writes
  it to `doc/`.
- `yardoc` queries the tags: `--query '@return && @param'`, `--query 'has_tag?(:api)'`.
- `bundle exec rake graph` prints the classes and modules as a Graphviz graph
  (`yard graph --protected --private --full --dependencies`): `| dot -Tsvg -o graph.svg`.

### 1.3. Types: signatures by hand, TypeProf to start them

`sig/` is written by hand and Steep holds `lib/` to it, so every method's types are there, a private
one's too (it has no YARD tags): look a method's up in `sig/<%= path %>.rbs`.
`bundle exec rake typeprof` prints the signatures TypeProf infers from `lib/`: a start for a new
method's, never a replacement for reading them -- it says `untyped` where it cannot tell. A
signature is as narrow as the code allows: `untyped` only where the value truly is any value. When
a diagnostic has to stay, ignore it on its line, the reason on the line above (text after the
diagnostic's name voids the ignore):

```ruby
# the library declares no signature
value = client.fetch # steep:ignore NoMethod
```

## 2. Comments: only what is essential

A comment says what the code cannot: why it is so, the source it follows, the constraint it keeps.
Make it concise and precise -- a line where a line does -- and never restate the code: that is
noise, and it goes stale. A change that falsifies a comment rewrites it.

## 3. Commits

[Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) are enforced by `cog check` locally
and in CI. The changelog is generated from the commits (`cliff.toml`), so the subject line is
user-facing text; `chore`, `ci` and `style` commits stay out of it, and `!` or a `BREAKING CHANGE`
footer lists a commit first:

```
feat: accept the name as a symbol too
fix: strip a trailing newline from the name
docs: explain the greeting's fallback
build(deps): depend on json
```

The git hooks (`bundle exec rake hooks`) check the same before a commit and a push; never bypass
them with `--no-verify`. `git revert` writes `Revert "..."`, which CI refuses: reword it as
`revert: ...`.

## 4. Before you claim a change is done

Run `bundle exec rake checks`. It runs CI's gates: RuboCop, Steep, the tests with every line,
branch and method covered, yard-lint, bundler-audit, dprint, the commit messages, the shell, the
workflows, and the flake's checks. Reporting "done" on a change that has not passed it is the
one thing that wastes the most time here.

If a check fails for reasons unrelated to your change, say so explicitly rather than working
around it -- a broken check that everyone routes around stops protecting anything.
